<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suppiluliuma I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1344–1322 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suppiluliuma I was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> who reigned c. 1344–1322 BCE. He expanded Hittite power into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>. He <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-sack-of-washukanni">sacked Washukanni</a> and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza">Shattiwaza</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i.jpg" alt="Suppiluliuma I" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Ikonact · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Hittite_rule_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Šuppiluliuma's origins are unclear. Seal impressions naming him as the son of Tudḫaliya III led most scholars to take him as that king's son, though his first wife Ḫenti is called the daughter of a great king, which has led some to think he was also Tudḫaliya's son-in-law and possibly adopted son. Before he became king he served as adviser and chief military commander. According to the Deeds of Šuppiluliuma, written by his son Muršili II, he helped recover Hittite lands from the Kaška in the north, defeated King Karanni of Azzi-Ḫayaša, and fought the Arzawa confederacy in the west, retaking the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyana">Tuwanuwa</a>.</p><p>Tudḫaliya III's heir, Tudḫaliya the Younger, had sworn oaths of loyalty from Šuppiluliuma and other nobles. He was murdered with some of his brothers by Šuppiluliuma's supporters, and Šuppiluliuma became king. An oracle later named the murder as a cause of the plague that afflicted the Hittite kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Šuppiluliuma set out to recover Kizzuwatna and Tegarama, which had come under the influence of Mittani. An early attack failed, so he allied with a rival Mittanian royal, Artatama II, and married a Babylonian princess. In the so-called One-Year War he overran Mittani's vassal Išuwa and plundered the capital Waššukanni, then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Mukiš, Qatna and other Syrian kingdoms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-ii">Niqmaddu II</a> of Ugarit accepted his suzerainty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> fell after its king Šutatarra gave battle.</p><p>In the Six-Year War, fought largely by his son Telipinu, he besieged and captured Carchemish and made his son Piyaššili its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king. Telipinu ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and the two princes served as viceroys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. When Šattiwaza, son of Tušratta, fled Mittani, Šuppiluliuma gave him a daughter in marriage and sent him with a Hittite army to take what remained of his father's kingdom, which became in effect a dependency, later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Late in his reign an Egyptian queen, usually identified as Ankhesenamun, asked him for one of his sons as husband and king. He sent Zannanza, who was killed on the way. Šuppiluliuma blamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, attacked Egyptian territory and took captives. Muršili II recorded that these prisoners brought a plague to the Hittite kingdom. The plague lasted at least two decades and is often considered the cause of the deaths of Šuppiluliuma and his eldest son Arnuwanda II. Muršili II blamed the kingdom's misfortune on his father's cruelty, counting the murder of Tudḫaliya the Younger and the attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> among its causes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Šuppiluliuma I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Hittite Kingdom reached its full strength under <b>Suppiluliuma I</b> (c. 1344–1322 BCE), who made the Hittites a great power competing directly with Egypt and Mitanni for control of the Levantine corridor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the 1340s BCE neighbouring kingdoms, including Mukish, Niya and Nuhashshe, attacked Ugarit when Niqmaddu II refused to join their coalition against the Hittites; he appealed to <b>Suppiluliuma I</b>, who drove the attackers off and rewarded Ugarit with territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its main conflicts were the wars with Egypt for control of Syria under Thutmose III, later replaced by alliance, and Tushratta's struggle against the Hittite king <b>Suppiluliuma I</b>, which led in the end to the sack of Washukanni around 1340 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…as Egypt declined: the Akkar Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of <b>Suppiluliuma I</b> of Hatti, and Tutankhamun and his general Horemheb scrambled to keep Egypt's hold on the southern Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Suppiluliuma I</b> of the Hittite Empire made a treaty with Hukkana of Hayasa and gave him his sister in marriage, with strict rules about Hittite customs.</blockquote><a href="/state/azzi-hayasa">Azzi-Hayasa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Suppiluliuma I</b> of the Hittite Empire conquered Isuwa in the 14th century BCE and made it a vassal.</blockquote><a href="/state/isuwa">Isuwa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.02,34.62&amp;year=-1330" aria-label="Hattusa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Suppiluliuma I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5302 4141h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5302 4141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.02,34.62&amp;year=-1330">What was here in 1330 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
